review:
This review is not about whether I liked this game or not – I did like it.
... Sort of.
This review is about: 'Would I [b]recommend[/b] it?'
Spoiler alert: no, I wouldn't – because 'I am future' is the epitome of 'great idea, poor execution': in there, 'cozy' unfortunately just means 'slow and tedious'.

The good:
- The core idea feels somewhat innovative;
- The world-building has some consistency;
- The AD is simple – but not simplistic. It conveys exactly what it needs to make the player feel relaxed;
- The music score changes at strategic moments (dawn, midday, dusk, sunset), allowing you to keep track of in-game time without having to think about it;
- I liked the 'story', and I liked the NPCs;
- I also liked the NPCs mini-games, especially that each has been designed relevantly for the NPC you get to play with;
- Minions are really cute!;
- The 'spraying' mechanic is well-made and feels really comfortable to use ;
- Just enough variety was provided with the appliances you get to dismantle (as well as just the right amount of them);
- Being able to paint the storage containers so we can color-code them – and seeing the chosen color showing next to the name of the container in the list when accessing the storage meta-network - is a great design choice;
- I liked that the tools at our disposal are so various;
- English translation is on point.

The bad (in no particular order):
- World-building really could have used more consistency ([spoiler]Are we a clone or an android? Please make up your mind... that's not the same at all[/spoiler] – [spoiler]Kindred spirit with Earl after what he pulled, with no narrative transition whatsoever?[/spoiler] – [spoiler]A machine selling food while there are no humans left on the planet?[/spoiler]);
- While music was used the right way, the uninspired musical score gets annoying real fast (forget about the cool music from the ad, you won't ever hear it in-game) ;
- It's really easy to get soft-locked: you get access to new areas and can clean them up almost entirely [b]before[/b] the story leads you there. You end up using materials that you have in limited supply, unaware of their rarity – and that you absolutely need them to unlock the next step in the story... Of course, you can demolish structures to get said materials back, so it's not a game-breaker. Still...
- Yes, there's a 'demolishing structure' option – but it is only accessible via a very discrete button at the bottom left of your construction panel (not intuitive);
- While you get to fish, the QTE becomes boring really fast;
- While you can connect all your storage containers together via the storage meta-network, navigating said meta-network is a real pain;
- The mechanic of picking up/dropping off large resources (metal sheets/planks/plexiglass) is really clunky:
1/ a mere pixel off when you're clicking to pick something up and you drop everything (also works when you unintentionally click somewhere you shouldn't);
2/ using the directional arrows while maintaining the right-button of your mouse clicked (so you can move the camera) often ends up in you unintentionally dropping off what you are carrying too;
- Large resources storages can't be upgraded, so you can't have storages containing more than 10 units of metal sheets/planks/plexiglass;
- Large resources storages are not connected to the storage meta-network, therefore can't be accessed from the manufacturing devices ;
- You can build solar generators and a power grid... but your kitchen will only use planks (wood) to work – forever;
- You get to discover recipes for many different dishes... but there's absolutely no use for them whatsoever in the long run;
- You get to fix the roof – but someone forgot to 'activate' tiles within some fixable roof patches;
- Someone also forgot to 'activate' dismantling for some items, so you get stuck with some pesky piles of paper and wooden structures on the ground... forever;
- Most of your structures will need power to work, so you get to build an electrical grid. To ease the connecting process between structures, you can build electrical poles. But their connecting radius is so low that you end up having to build way more than feels necessary;
- Moreover, electrical poles' 'connect mode' is automatically cancelled when you get too far – and the radius being so low, it's often impossible to connect poles located at different levels: you end up automatically exiting the 'connect mode' because you have to make a detour to take stairs;
- You will need water for your crops and for cooking, and the most convenient way to get some is from water pumps. But you can't freely place water pumps... And you don't get one on every roof, while having no way of transporting water from one point to another other than by hand (... with a bucket, three units at a time...) – so forget the idea of building a farm just anywhere;
- You get to pilot a drone to explore points of interest over the map, in what's called 'expedition mode'. But there's no indicator of the drone's battery level outside of 'expedition mode'. Can't remember how much battery left your drone has? Endure another loading screen to access 'expedition mode' again;
- You can tear down just about anything... but can only recycle a selected handful of items;
- You get a nano transporter to move structures around, but only one structure at a time. Try and move your entire farm(s), one garden bed at a time – then pray for an upgrade allowing players to transport multiple units of the same structure (that will never come: the nano transporter can't be upgraded like other tools can);
- You can customize minions, but the available colors are not saturated enough – making most of them barely distinguishable from one another once applied.

The ugly:
- Sometimes, the game camera randomly enters 'ecstasy-addict mode' – it starts to frantically spin around your character for a couple seconds, for no particular reason (... if at least there was disco music playing when it does that, that could have been funny but... nope, there ain't) ;
- We have access to a nano transporter. Although... we also have to manually pick up large resources and can only transport 6 units max at any given time ;
- Said nano transporter can pick up bushes and trees, no problem – but moving a wild lettuce plant? Nope, can't do that... – there really should have had upgrades for the nano transporter ;
- While you do have to provide power to a lot of structures for them to work, elevators do not need nor use power... they're magical!
- Since elevators don't need nor use power, you can't connect them to any existing power grid – meaning if you want to start expanding to an area accessible only by elevator, you need to build a whole new power grid there;
- Since minions can't use elevators nor teleporters... same point as above ;
- Speaking of minions: they're a useless hindrance – slow, stupid, and they get stuck everywhere – I had written more about them, but Steam keeps pestering me my review is too long -_- (they're really cute though!);
- The sprinklers watering radius is so low that once you've packed together your 24 garden beds (+ the cables to connect them together) within the sprinkler's area of effect, you end up unable to see anything at all (and close to unable to efficiently click on anything too) ;
- Teleporters really feel like a late, not very well-thought addition. A quick band-aid to address a 'bigger than planned for' map. Bonus: they don't need electricity either...

Conclusion:
I spent a lot of time moving everything around – and testing everything that came to mind – so you wouldn't have to. Knowing what I know now, I wouldn't buy the game again.
So, as announced: no, I can't recommend it.
If you like this vibe, get yourself 'No place like home' instead.

(Yes, I realize this is a passion project coming from an indie studio. That's why there are more things I could have listed, but ultimately have chosen not to.)

The game has potential. Unfortunately, it wasn't tested enough for UI and UX:

1) You can't switch between fullscreen and windowed mode once you are on the map screen. You have to go back to the main menu screen to do that;
2) There is no defined "Enter the base" button while on the map screen. So it's easy to misclick "Move the base" and "Enter the base" on-map buttons;
3) It's very easy to misclick 1 ( first special ability ) and Q ( melee attack ), thus wasting the special ability, when you just wanted to attack. And there is no button remapping in the settings;
4) There is a problem with unit overlapping. You might be aiming at one unit with a special ability but instead hit another one, just below it;
5) End of turn is entirely random. Sometimes you are allowed to end a turn, sometimes the game just ends it for you. This should have been an option;
6) Sometimes enemy units just freeze and become completely unresponsive. Saving and loading the game doesn't help. You have to abandon the battle that you've spent a considerable amount of time on...

I've been a big fan of RPG Maker games ever since WItch House-Ao Oni-etc came out, and while I can't say this was the best game I've ever seen, House of Rules has it's lovely bits - as well as critical flaws.

+Atmosphere is pretty solid. Eerie haunted mansion can make you nervous from time to time, the music is well fitting, there's nothing really falling out of place here;
+A few interesting takes on gameplay: I loved the 'hints' concept, i.e.;
+Puzzles. I usually have hard time playing games that include puzzles, but this game nailed it pretty well. They are not too hard (except for the godforsaken sheep puzzle) and fun: you gotta think, but not *that* hard.
+Interesting characters and dialogues. It was pretty interesting to delve into their stories, even though the characters themselves weren't fleshed out the best;

+-A 'classic' story. A lot of clichees. Literally everywhere. Yeah, dolls, I'm looking at you. Sure, it helps to build a good surrounding and an engaging story, but... If you're looking for something fresh, for something that will blow your head - nuh-uh, not here;
+-You get a lot of endings - you get a lot of achievments. There's a lot of endings, but aiming to see/get them all will quickly leave you disappointed, as it's not as exciting as it seems. Literally ~3 out of 20 is worth replaying the same scenes;

-Graphics. I know that you can see it on the screenshots, but it doesn't mean characters' faces, blurry/low-res pictures etc won't make your eyes bleed;
-Chasing sequences. Hella annoying, can make you replay the same scene for a dozen times because the enemy is fast and the controls, which are pretty fine when NOTHING IS CHASING YOU, are wacky;
-Clunky UI. Nuff said, not the most efficient design;

Can't say it was a memorable experience. You can enjoy House of Rules, but you will forget about it as soon as you close the game.
Overall it's definitely a 5/10. Can't recommend, can't warn against.

This small game is a nice little homage both to the early 2000's era of edgy flash games and to the oldschool bio-mecha anime akin to Evangelion or Guyver. While not presenting too much in terms of level design or enemy behavior, it has very tight and responsible controls, and allows player to become a killing machine, cutting through everything like a hot knife. The levels don't have any special objectives besides finding the exit or killing the boss, so it's up to you - to wipe out every location of the enemies, or just rush straight to the exit.
Aside from simple, but satisfying combat, the game also presents such an interesting feature as eating the corpses of your enemies. This will either heal the Cynoroid, or replenish the energy needed for performing the melee attacks/deflecting/acceleration mode.

In terms of difficulty, the game is quite easy even on the Hard mode, and I can't imagine many people dying even once during the first Act. With the appearance of the fast and sneaky enemies from the Act 2 the difficulty starting to ramp up a bit, and culminating in the three last levels of the Act 3, with all the fat enemies and not much ammo laying around. Even so, Cynoroid: Forsaken is a casual player friendly game, that is not a bad thing in this particular case. After all, isn't the Cynoroid supposed to be a badass mecha exterminator?

+ Advantages: responsible controls; satisfying and juicy combat full of blood and guro; murky post apocalyptic cyberpunk setting; easy to perform deflecting of enemy attacks; secret passages that could contain ammo; new skills, or some stuff from the dev;

- Disadvantages: medium lagging in big areas with many sounds overlaying each other; weird shooting sound of the automatic rifle (weapon 2); the level design doesn't present much diversity of backgrounds, traps, or environment;

For those who love edgy games stained with blood and adorned with intestines, like Zombie Smashers X or Rework the Dead: Evil, I recommend this one.

This is a scam game, don't be fooled by exquisite screenshots.

1. The control settings are anti human, with one English prompt requiring a button to be set once, and there are no custom settings for the UI interface;

The English prompt was very confusing and I couldn't understand what the custom button function was. I tested it myself for 15 minutes to find out which function to use;

2. AI is very mentally disabled. When I was motionless, AI waited for several seconds before taking the initiative to hit me a few times. To be disappointing, AI will not automatically combo. Even closing my eyes, i could still defeat AI when only held down one attack button. In addition, this game doesn't have a game difficulty setting;

3. I looked at the discussion group, but the developers didn't reply players yet. The game is dead;

Those positive reviews didn't tell the truth at all.

I don't recommend buying this game. There is a problem with the button settings, and you will definitely regret it.

Truly a masterpiece of the "dectective/investigator" genre.

My highlights:
- The story is actually done well and is involving;
- Diverisified puzzles that often made me feel smart when I thought outside the box, and the best part of the game for me? The [spoiler]BOMB DEFUSAL[/spoiler] puzzle.
- Extremely high quality (visuals, VA, sounds, music);
- The music;
- Interesting achievements that encourage replayability;
- Replayability: Immersion is not "broken" by players being all-knowing after an initial playthrough.

The things that I liked less:
- Too short. I understand designing the puzzles, all the visuals, doing all the research, the voice acting, etc. is incredibly time consuming, though;
- Some story beats were interesting only for the main character, but since the story is pretty short, I didn't have that same connection with some characters, so some beats were off for me;

Sparking Zero is more or less what i ve been wishing for in years; a sequel to the sparking/budokai tenkaichi series with characters and transformations from Dragon ball super added. The combat system is there with some interesting new additions, the graphics is gourgeous and many characters and transformations are accurate.
There are however some things that in my opinion could have been better:
- The story mode lacks a lot of important battles as far as the characters involved concerns. Vegeta , for example, doesn´t go as far as the end of the buu saga, when in reality his story should have reached the end of the tournament of power. I guess they thought they could get away with that with custom battle mode, where you can create your own battles with scenes and written dialogues which brings me to the next point;
- Custom battles are a nice touch, too bad that they forgot to give us something that could have really allowed us to create and recreate battles not featured in the story mode: MORE MAPS;
- Yes, the maps, i believe we got too few of them , especially compared to bt3. There are just too many of them missing such as the glacier ( which could allow us to recreate the last broly movie), kame house ( too iconic not to be in the game), outer space ( you know, just in case someone would like beerus and ssj god goku to fight there), planet vegeta, the new red ribbon base from super hero and so on. Even the dlc´s dont seem they will add more of them;
- Some characters and transformations are inexplicably missing; Why did they remove super 17? he was a major villain in dbgt, so why give GT a representation if you re not going to include him? I know they removed almost every great ape because a lot of them were not canon, too bad they removed one that actually happened in the anime and manga: Kid Goku´s: Why?;
- The OST is decent i guess but the anime OST DLC packs are way too expensive for what they offer. The japanese version of bt3 ( sparking meteor) had all of them from the get go, why do we have to pay for a track list that is incomplete since it s missing a lot of cool themes from dbs and dbgt?

Extended version of agai.io game ;}
Sandbox with beautiful graphic. A lot of variations of creating and buidling your cell
Create, evolve and explore this world. Good time killer

For the purchase price, you get:

+ A choose-your-own-party dungeon crawling experience;
+ Integrated cartography mechanics;
+ A sailing minigame;
+ A lovely soundtrack;
+ Fantastic artwork;
+ Simple controls;
+ Rich worldbuilding;
+ And hours and hours of fun (including postgame content!).

Potential detractors might be:

- Absolutely brutal difficulty (although this can be changed to be a cakewalk);
- Getting all the classes requires at least two playthroughs (though New Game+ exists);
- The game is a huge grinding timesink (especially on higher difficulties);
- Changing/modifying party members requires time and resources;
- The cartography controls are clunky (the original game made use of the Nintendo DS dual screens and was objectively better for it);
- The in-game font is atrocious (though this can be fixed with mods).

[b]Long-Form Review:[/b]

I am no stranger to Etrian Odyssey (I started with EO4), and I absolutely adore this series, as it's a comfortable blanket of potential. The general gist with EO is that you can build your own party from a series of classes, then take them on an adventure while manually mapping each floor of the dungeon you traverse. The plot is reasonably engaging, but the hooks to these games are the gameplay and the cartography elements.

Out of the three titles available on Steam at the time of this writing (EO 1, 2, and 3), this one is by far and away the best given that EO3 introduced new mechanics (such as subclassing and weapon crafting) which broadened the horizons of customization, min-maxing, and varied builds and veered away from the cookie-cutter party compositions of previous titles. There's also an overworld minigame of sorts that involves sailing, but true overworld exploration doesn't happen until EO4 (and is then never seen again).

This game was originally a Nintendo DS title, and like a certain amount of niche titles of the era, the difficulty was cranked up to be absolutely brutal by this era's standards. The remaster has introduced two easier difficulties (Normal and the ridiculously simple Picnic), but if you want the authentic experience, play on Expert. Even on Normal, however, you will need to build your party to have answers to problems that enemies pose, and if you don't have those, your only reward and basis for growth is death. Can't cleanse or block status effect spam? You're dead. Can't keep a boss's limbs perpetually bound to keep them from using the party-wide damage skill? You're dead. Can't dodge the roaming bosses that show up throughout the dungeon floors? Guess what, you're dead. You'll even die to random mobs if you have just one unfortunate encounter, and you'll need to prepare to prevent it, because the game is going to pull no punches right from the start.

There are ways to respec your party and even swap characters out, but understand that respecing costs a character five entire levels, you will need to train up any new characters from level one, and XP is a precious resource: any XP you get through battles is split among your entire party and will take time - [i]lots[/i] of time - to grind back. Money, too, will always be tight given that costs for new armor and weapons will massively increase over time, and you are going to have to hash out what you want to take with you for emergencies or otherwise. This game encourages deep thinking, party crafting, and planning, and although that's a turn-off for some, I wouldn't have it any other way.

That being said, this game definitely isn't worth full price, especially with Denuvo attached to it to tell you that you can't play your own games if you aren't connected to the Internet for long enough. Buy it on sale and enjoy the experience so that Atlus can release the rest of the series for us!

Not my usual cuppa tea. I generally go for point and click mystery games. This is a match 3 game with a bit of hidden objects. It follows the story of a serial killer that drains the victims of their blood. You as Detective Elliot need to search the crime scenes and follow leads. Each stage you have to complete several match 3 games to progress and arm yourself with bonus weapons that can aid you in your gameplay. All of it came to an abrupt stop for me when, hitting play, a prompt appeared that told me I should buy the upgrade version or so. Ah well, it is fun as it is. 

PROS:
- Difficulty variety of match 3 games;
- Simple Hidden Object scenes;
- Interesting story;
- Wonderful music;
- Cute match 3 objects;
- Easy to learn mechanics.

CONS:
- I would prefer no upgrades of any sorts. The whole game, please!
- How can I use the other aiding weapons instead of the magnifying glass, axe and stake?

All in all, a good game. I recommend it for the match 3 games. They are a delight! The music and the cute match 3 objects (bat, web, candles, axe, holy water, crucifix, etc.) is worth playing for!

I would recomend this game only if you want something cheap and a singleplayer dinosaur "simulator" experience (stopping to think now, I think many of these recent dinosaur simulator games are multiplayer, having something that is singlerplayer, like this one, I think is good). This game sure has some improvements if compared to the frist one, but it also have the same problems of the frist game:

- The game doesn't have a tutorial, which can be a problem to new players;

- I think the game could have some type of minimap,  because I got lost many times while trying to find the pods (Just to not get lost, I always walk across the edge of the map);

- The Scan seems to do nothing, it doesn't show me food or water, making me to find these myself;

- The new way to unlock the dinosaurus I think is cool (in the older one, you would need to find the Mutant version of the dinosaur, then kill it, and then you would unlock it. Thanks to that method, I've never unlocked the Therizinosaurus, because the map feels all the same). In this sequel, now to unlock new dinosaurus, you need to destroy a certain amount of pods. I like this, but then again, since the map feel the same, I think is somewhat hard to find these pods;

- The dinosaurus in this game will chase you for the rest of their lifes. You can only escape them by fighting them or using a really fast dinosaur, like the desert version of Appalachiosaurus (one of the stater dinosaurus, and the desert version already come along with it), if you spam the jump (which is the Left Alt by default) with it, you can basically escape from everything. This guy is also a good way to travel in the maps;

- This game explain its lore, but for some reason, you can find the lore of this game only in the description of the game in the Steam page, because in the game itself, never explains the lore, the pods and the mutants just exists.

For the pros, it does have some new dinosaurus, like Megalosaurus, Allosaurus, Kentrosaurus and, the one I liked the most (just because it is brazilian) Oxalaia. 
The game now have Alpha and Apex versions for some dinosaurus, which have a significant better stats than the normals ones (or worse, as the moment I'm writing this, I just got the Alpha Plateosaurus, and it is almost a better Plateosaurus, but it is slower than the normal one). 
The way the camera is positioned reminds me of Disney's Dinosaur PC/PS2 game, which I like it.

I have no idea if the developer of this game will keep woking on it, since he/she is woking in a other dinosaur game (Dinosaurs Dominion), but I think is game is good just to have a little fun.

Edit: I just finished the game, and after I've destroy all the pods, I have unlocked the final map, which is a boss battle against a big mutant Tiktaalik (an animal that is the transition from fish to amphibian) and a giant pod, the Mother Pod, which is just a larger pod with more health. And after I had beaten the Tiktaalik and destroyed the Mother Pod, I just won the game, I didn't even get any reward, which I think is a shame, because I have spend 7,2 hours playing this game (I've spend that many hours just trying to find the pods, even with the help of the desert Carnotaurus, which I think it is the fastest dinosaur in the game).

[I] The Hepatica Spring [/i] is a nice lighthearted novel. It's a neutral review. Nothing exciting about the plot or the whole experience but it was an entertaining enough distraction I needed. 

Things I enjoyed:
+great color choices, a real feast for my eyes, very relaxing atmosphere (that's the reason enough to choose a thumb-up review);
+wonderfully drawn sprites, so it's really a pity the quality of CGs don't match, for my taste;
https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=2769946281
+captivating but still light plot, perfect amount of suspense and drama;
+good price for the story;
+all routes and two possible outcomes (love and friendship) are well-written, there was absolutely nothing to dislike in any ending! Even that obligatory girl x girl route. Sorry, not a fan. But it was fine. Hmm, you know, rn scanning through the store page I found out “Play as any gender you please. MC has no canon gender” :)) I even restarted the game thinking maybe I forgot I could choose my gender, nope I guess I misunderstood and it just means in the story no one addressed MC directly with any specific gender. But dunno, I still felt MC was a girl, maybe it's my native lan's influence as the offered name 'Mika' sounds like female for me having that 'a' ending. 

Smth to whine a little :D
--typos;
--not enough save slots :(((;
--for me the game was lagging. For example, old CGs disappear from the gallery when I open a new one or overwriting saves didn't work at some point;
--not a fan of blurry/murky CGs;
https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=2794275160 
--I didn't get enough visual part from this VN :(( I could read and read for some time and just backgrounds :(( no sprites. When chars talk, I'd really like to look at them;
--quite often the emotions described and the ones portrayed on the chars' sprites didn't match;
--moving bgs really hurt my eyes, I hate when it happens in novels :((
--welp, maybe it's just me and depressed mood I had while playing but the chars as usual annoyed me a lot! 

So, all in all, it's a pleasant enough romantic fantasy novel for a couple of evenings (don't mind my time on record).

